{
  "headline": "Cadence's fundamentals show real promise — 75% gross margin and default-alive trajectory at base growth — but the model is too sparse and stale to withstand investor scrutiny today.",
  "investor_talking_points": [
    "Our gross margin is 75%, at the KeyBanc SaaS benchmark for our stage, and our burn multiple is 2.1x — within the acceptable range for a seed company investing ahead of scale.",
    "At our base-case 8% monthly growth rate, our revenue curve crosses breakeven before we run out of cash — we are default-alive.",
    "We have $1.5M in cash and have identified the key model gaps — headcount plan, CAC, forward projections — that we are now building out to share with investors."
  ],
  "lenses": {
    "runway": {
      "callout": "Default-alive at 8% MoM growth: in the base scenario, revenue growth outpaces the $120K/month burn before cash runs out.",
      "highlight": "Downside runway is 10–13 months (crisis: April 2027, slow: July 2027) — tight but not immediately critical if fundraising begins now.",
      "watch_out": "The threshold scenario requires maintaining at least 5.5% MoM growth. Any growth deceleration below that level converts the company from default-alive to cash-constrained within 18 months."
    },
    "unit_economics": {
      "callout": "Gross margin of 75% is strong and exactly at the KeyBanc seed-stage SaaS benchmark — a genuine investor talking point.",
      "watch_out": "CAC is entirely absent from the model. Without CAC, LTV/CAC and payback period cannot be computed — these are the first questions any SaaS investor will ask. The implied burn multiple of 2.1x against Mar 2025 data (14x if computed off $102K net new ARR) is a significant red flag that the model cannot currently explain."
    }
  }
}

